# Quipu Primitive Canon v1.1 — Knots, Strings, Tombstones

Issued by Datachain Foundation SAS. Canonical. Supersedes Quipu Primitive Canon v1.0 and all prior block-related terminology on Datachain Rope and Datawallet+ surfaces.

## 1. Core Vocabulary (mandatory)

| Legacy term | Canonical Datachain Rope term |
|---|---|
| block | **knot** |
| block height / block number | **knot height** / **knot index** |
| genesis block | **genesis knot** |
| block reward | **knot reward** |
| block time | **knot interval** |
| block producer / miner | **knot witness** (modern post-quantum quipucamayoq) |
| finality | **knot anchoring** (at federation level) |
| chain (per-wallet ledger) | **string** (the per-wallet sovereign micro-ledger) |
| transaction erasure (NEW) | **rope_untieKnot** producing a **tombstone knot** |

"Block" is **deprecated** in user-facing and protocol-facing language. Use "knot" everywhere except in the EVM-compat alias layer (see §3).

## 2. The Two Paradoxes Resolved

1. **Block-mention paradox** — sovereign-thread architecture cannot share a "block" between strangers. Each wallet owns a sovereign **string**; events on that string are **knots** tied on the cord, not slots in a globally-shared container.
2. **Personal-ledger erasure paradox** — until v1.1, the smallest GDPR-addressable unit was the entire string. Users invoking GDPR Art. 17 had a binary choice: nuke the whole wallet history or keep the targeted event. The knot primitive makes events **individually addressable and individually erasable**, surgically, while preserving all unrelated attestations / deeds / credentials / balances on the same string.

## 3. JSON-RPC Surface (mandatory mapping)

Canonical methods on the Datachain Rope node:

```text
rope_knotIndex                          ← canonical (replaces eth_blockNumber)
rope_getKnotByIndex(index, fullTxs)     ← canonical (replaces eth_getBlockByNumber)
rope_getKnotByHash(hash, fullTxs)       ← canonical (replaces eth_getBlockByHash)
rope_untieKnot(stringId, eventId)       ← NEW; no EVM equivalent; produces a tombstone knot
```

EVM-compat aliases (kept indefinitely for tooling, MetaMask, ethers.js, etc.):

```text
eth_blockNumber           → returns rope_knotIndex
eth_getBlockByNumber      → wraps rope_getKnotByIndex
eth_getBlockByHash        → wraps rope_getKnotByHash
```

The EVM aliases must continue to work; they return the same data, just under the legacy name. New SDK code should call the `rope_*` methods.

## 4. The Knot — First-Class Object Schema

Every knot tied on a string MUST carry at minimum:

```text
event_id        — deterministic, string-scoped knot index (position along the cord)
event_hash      — cryptographic commitment over the knot payload (anchored at federation)
event_type      — transfer | mint | burn | attestation | claim | upload | inference | erasure | ...
event_size      — payload magnitude (long-knot turn-count analogue)
event_payload   — encrypted under the string's sovereign key material (OES key shred for erasure)
event_signature — post-quantum (ML-DSA-65 / Dilithium3 default; SLH-DSA permitted)
```

## 5. Controlled Erasure / RDP Protocol (rope_untieKnot)

When `rope_untieKnot(string_id, event_id)` is invoked (or its SDK wrapper `datawallet.untie(event_id)`):

1. **Cryptographic destruction** — the knot's encrypted payload key is shredded under OES (Organic Encryption System), making the underlying personal data permanently unreadable. This satisfies EDPB cryptographic-erasure guidance.
2. **Tombstone preserved** — the knot's *position* on the string remains as a deliberate absence (a **tombstone knot**), with timestamp + authorization proof but no recoverable payload.
3. **String integrity preserved** — hash continuity, knot index of subsequent knots, and all unrelated attestations/deeds/credentials/balances are **unaffected**.
4. **Federation anchoring** — the untying is recorded as a first-class auditable event on the federation cord. Audit trails survive; payloads do not.
5. **Irreversible in fact** — not even the Foundation, validators, or the user can recover the erased payload.

**Compliance scope**: GDPR Art. 17, CCPA, LGPD, PIPL, POPIA, EU AI Act, DORA. Position this as a **regulatory moat** — no Bitcoin / Ethereum / EVM L1/L2 / Cosmos / Polkadot chain offers native granular erasure.

**Critical**: untying erases personal-data payload; it does **not** reverse a financial transfer. Financial reversal is a separate operation.

